 "conclusions": [
  "Taking a position from the broadcast stream where both cameras have it and agree does not help: the ball -0.001 (sharp), +0.001 (good), -0.005 (medium); ball and players +0.002, 0.000, -0.004; always the broadcast ball -0.002 / -0.005; the mean of the two balls -0.005 / 0.000. This holds for a stream five times as precise as the tactical copy (ball error 0.03 m against 0.15 m; players 0.04 m against 0.13 m). Where two measurements agree within half a metre, choosing between them changes little, and precision below the level the models were trained with is not what limits them.",
  "The second camera matters through what the first does not see: without it the score is 0.036-0.043 lower on these copies (all 8 lower). On the simulated copies the tactical ball is there on 46% of graded frames, the broadcast ball adds 15-17%, and on 38-40% neither camera has it; both have it on 22-25%.",
  "Players that the tactical copy has lost and the broadcast stream sees: adding them (F) gained +0.0057 (sharp) and +0.0097 (good) on four copies, and +0.0069 with a stream that itself loses and invents players, where only 41-67% of the added positions are real players. 9 of the 12 comparisons better, worst -0.0027. Replacing positions as well (PF) was not clearly better and moves tactical positions.",
  "F was registered as a candidate with the gates of the earlier plans (possession-v216-plan.json)."
 ],
 "limits": [
  "Four development copies per comparison (eight for B1 and P in two regimes); differences under about 0.005 are within the spread between copies.",
